misc: hy310-board-mgr: say why unload leaves the fan rail on, now without a hog

remove() said "GPIO hog keeps it running", and hy310_fan_power_en() that
fan power "is normally managed by DTS gpio-hog". On the HY310 the board
file now hands PB5 over without a hog, and suspend switches it low on
purpose. Read as they stood, both comments invite a later change that
puts the hog back or never switches the rail.

The comments now say what holds: a hogged line stays as it is, a rail
handed over keeps its level when devm releases the request (the sunxi
pinctrl free leaves the pin configuration alone), only suspend and
resume switch it, and unload does not cut it because on other boards the
fan may still have a light to cool.

Comments only; the module builds to the same code. Compiled out of tree
with W=1, no new warning.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/drivers/misc/hy310-board-mgr.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/hy310-board-mgr.c
@@ -390,11 +390,10 @@ static int hy310_fan_power_en(struct hy310_board_mgr *priv, int enable)
 			continue;
 
 		/*
-		 * Fan power GPIO is normally managed by DTS gpio-hog.
-		 * We only override it on suspend (off) / resume (on).
-		 * gpio_request_one + gpio_free to avoid permanent ownership.
+		 * A rail handed to us by the board file is switched directly.
+		 * Otherwise a short request, freed again at once; it fails
+		 * while a gpio-hog holds the line, which then stays as it is.
 		 */
-		/* A rail handed to us by the board file is switched directly. */
 		if (fan->power_owned) {
 			gpio_set_value(fan->power_gpio, enable);
 			switched++;
@@ -1448,8 +1447,13 @@ static void hy310_board_mgr_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&priv->fan_work);
 	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&priv->ntc_work);
 
-	/* Do NOT disable fan power on unload - GPIO hog keeps it running.
-	 * Cutting fan power risks thermal damage to the projector. */
+	/*
+	 * Fan power is left as it is on unload. A hog keeps its line; a rail
+	 * the board file hands over (the HY310's PB5, which also feeds the LED
+	 * boost) keeps its level when devm releases the request. Only suspend
+	 * switches it off, and resume on again. Cutting it here could stop a
+	 * fan that still has a light to cool.
+	 */
 
 	/* PWMs are devm-managed, no manual free needed */
 
